    "evidence_text": "DNA Deamination Mediates Innate Immunity to Retroviral Infection\n\nEfavirenz, a tight binding inhibitor, restricts the influence of the mutation.\n\nIt is the main driving force for the generation of drug resistance and the escape from immune surveillance.\n\nThis effect can be counteracted by the HIV-1 virion infectivity factor (Vif).\n\nA novel molecular mechanism of dual resistance to nucleoside and nonnucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors.\n\nN348I in the Connection Domain of HIV-1 Reverse Transcriptase Confers Zidovudine and Nevirapine Resistance\n\nAlmost all known RT inhibitor resistance mutations identified to date map to the polymerase domain of the enzyme.\n\nConclusions\n\nHowever, the mechanism(s) involved in the blockage of reverse transcription remains undefined.\n\nIn this study we investigated whether nucleotide levels influence viral reverse transcription in G0 cells.\n\nFurthermore, the replication capacity of HIV-1 containing I132M is severely impaired.\n\nWe treated 15 ATLL patients with IFN and AZT.\n\nHypercalcaemia predicted a poor outcome but differences were not significant.\n\nIn conclusion, IFN and AZT improves the outcome of ATLL patients and helps maintain responses.",
